    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d431">

                <pb facs="0451" n="447" sameAs="frus:frus1938v03/pg447"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.943 Manchuria/51: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Japan</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-02-24T18:00:00-05:00">February 24, 1938—6
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>68. Your 93, February 9, 10 p.m., via Shanghai.<anchor n="93" xml:id="d431-f1"/>
                    Department feels that, as representations in regard to some of the laws
                    mentioned in your telegram under reference have been made, and as our general
                    position with regard to laws of this kind which violate the “Open Door”
                    principle has repeatedly been brought to the attention of the authorities at
                    Hsinking and at Tokyo, and as we have registered at both places emphatic
                    objection to any attempt on the part of the authorities of Manchuria to exercise
                    jurisdiction over American nationals and made full reservation with regard to
                    the treaty rights of the United States and its citizens, no useful purpose would
                    be served by further action by this Government at this time.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d431-f1" n="93" xml:id="d431-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it reported British representations to the Japanese Government on
                    the subject of treaty rights in Manchuria.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
